    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d913">

                <pb facs="1188" n="1178" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1178"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.3516/7–1846: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-07-24T12:00:00+03:00">July 24,
                            1946—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received July 24—8:07 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2265. I addressed letter on July 23 to Lozovski requesting clarification of his
                    letter of July 12 as to how Embassy might send messages <pb facs="1189" n="1179"
                        xml:id="pg1179"/> to Dairen through Soviet military communication
                    facilities. I also stated that I assumed coded messages are acceptable for
                    transmission.</p>
                <p>We are attempting to send through regular commercial channels to Dairen text of
                    Lozovski’s July 12 letter in Brown Code. We are also acknowledging in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">en clair</hi> telegram to Dairen receipt of Dairen’s No.
                    4, July 8, which was sent to Dept in Embtel 2204, July 17.</p>
                <p>What codes does Benninghoff have?</p>
                <p>Dept please repeat to Nanking as Moscow’s 93.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
